    <header class="mb-8">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 bg-stone-100 border border-stone-300 rounded-full text-stone-800 text-xs font-semibold mb-3">
        <span>🏗️ Materials Science &amp; Masonry</span>
        <span>•</span>
        <span>ACI Technical Guide</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-3 tracking-tight">
        Concrete Mix Ratios Explained: 3000 vs 4000 PSI &amp; Curing Chemistry
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        Adding too much water to a concrete mix makes it easy to pour, but cuts its structural strength in half and causes surface dusting. Here is how concrete mix ratios and chemical hydration work.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Interactive Tool Callout -->
    <div class="p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-indigo-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl mb-8 shadow-xs flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-start sm:items-center justify-between gap-4">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-base font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Calculate concrete volume and bag counts</h2>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600">Convert length, width, and slab thickness into cubic yards and 60-lb or 80-lb pre-mixed bags.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="/calculators/concrete-slab" class="shrink-0 inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs font-bold px-4 py-2.5 rounded-lg transition-colors shadow-2xs">
        <span>Open Concrete Calculator</span>
        <span>→</span>
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">1. The Four Ingredients of Concrete</h2>
      <p>
        Concrete is not cement. <strong>Portland cement</strong> is merely the binding powder (representing roughly 10% to 15% of the mix). When mixed with water, sand (fine aggregate), and crushed gravel (coarse aggregate), a chemical reaction begins that transforms the slurry into synthetic stone.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">2. Standard Mix Ratios by Volume</h2>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose mb-6">
        <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 shadow-xs">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-700 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">STANDARD FOOTINGS &amp; PATIOS</div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">1:2:3 Mix (3,000 PSI)</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong>1 part Cement : 2 parts Sand : 3 parts Gravel</strong>. Ideal for pedestrian sidewalks, garden patios, shed slabs, and residential wall footings. Withstands 3,000 pounds per square inch of compression.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 shadow-xs">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-700 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">VEHICLE SLABS &amp; DRIVEWAYS</div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">1:1.5:2.5 Mix (4,000 PSI)</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong>1 part Cement : 1.5 parts Sand : 2.5 parts Gravel</strong>. Higher cement density gives dense, low-porosity concrete capable of supporting heavy pickup trucks and resisting freeze-thaw spalling.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">3. The Critical Water-to-Cement (W/C) Ratio</h2>
      <p>
        The single biggest mistake in DIY concrete is adding too much water. Complete chemical hydration only requires approximately <strong>0.25 to 0.30 pounds of water per pound of cement</strong>. Additional water added for "workability" is excess water.
      </p>
      <p>
        As that excess water bleeds to the surface and evaporates, it leaves millions of microscopic capillary pores throughout the cured concrete. A water-cement ratio of 0.45 yields 4,500 PSI concrete; increasing the water-cement ratio to 0.65 cuts strength down to 2,500 PSI.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">4. Hydration Curing Chemistry: 7 vs 28 Days</h2>
      <p>
        Concrete does not "dry" — it <strong>hydrates</strong>. Moisture must be trapped inside the slab to allow calcium silicate hydrate crystals to interlock:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Day 1 to 3:</strong> Initial set; slab reaches ~40% of ultimate design strength.</li>
        <li><strong>Day 7:</strong> Reaches approximately <strong>70% of design strength</strong> (safe for light foot traffic).</li>
        <li><strong>Day 28:</strong> Reaches <strong>100% full specified compressive strength (PSI)</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Spraying the slab with water daily or covering it with plastic sheeting for the first 7 days increases final strength by up to 50% compared to allowing it to dry out in the wind and sun.
      </p>
    </div>

